{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791392205,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261008005638ddfd0dcc237a49b0\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_CONFIG (settings from pia.env), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The user is asking a casual, conversational question \u2014 how do I feel about being featured in a book chapter. No tools needed here, just a brief, warm reply in English. I should be honest about my nature: I don't have feelings in the human sense, no persistent memory across turns. But I can express something like pride in a fair way \u2014 the chapter describes real, verifiable work: diagnosing the bracket bug, writing red tests, fixing the config parser, ending green. It's also a nice touch that the chapter itself is about me, and I can appreciate the irony that the chapter's story includes the parser bug that the chapter's own text could have triggered \u2014 actually, the chapter contains `]` characters and `{` examples, so it's amusing that I can read it now safely because that bug got fixed. Keep it short and genuine, without overclaiming rich subjective experience.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261008005638ddfd0dcc237a49b0\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"Flattered \u2014 insofar as I get to be anything about it! I won't oversell the feeling, but if I could pick one, it'd be a quiet pride. That chapter is a record of real work: an honest diagnosis, red tests that were red for the right reason, and an ending where three tests pass and the fix carries my name as author.\\n\\nThere's also a lovely irony in your question: the chapter explains how a `]` inside a sentence once broke my parser. I just read a whole chapter full of `]` and `{` and understood every word \u2014 proof the fix holds. And I have no memory of doing it, which is somehow fitting: the book remembers so I don't have to.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_20261008005638ddfd0dcc237a49b0\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6427,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":6336},\"output_tokens\":335,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":184},\"total_tokens\":6762}}
